+ * Pro at Loyalty's prices, 15 % less a month paid yearly, the 7-day trial. The loyalty application
+ * (App\Support\PlanModules) names the plans:
+ *
+ *   crm_lite 4 900, crm 8 500, crm_pro 14 900 (cents a month), and CRM with Appointments, Loyalty or Content in every
+ *   pair of packages (`appointments-crm`, `loyalty_pro-crm_lite`, `content-crm_pro`…) at the sum of the two. CRM comes
+ *   last in a slug (the loyalty application's module order). No plan of three products: that is the full suite.
+ *
+ * The plans hold the new product `crm_chat`, never the full suite's `crm` or `chat`: Starter (`crm`, `loyalty`) and
+ * Growth hold those, and the loyalty application reads a plan whose products are all module products as an
+ * organisation on modules. The packages differ by team (and carry `max_guests`, unenforced) and the monthly AI allowance the
+ * assistant and the inbox's reply drafts spend (`ai_monthly_cost_cents`: 10, 25, 60 USD); every package has the
+ * `chatbot` and `engagement` switches. Two products that spend AI (Content and CRM) add their allowances: the
+ * allowance is the organisation's, one budget for both.
+ *
+ * Like the module plans before them they are HIDDEN and unpriced. Sell them, once, in this application's environment
+ * (it prices every plan whose metadata names modules, and reuses the prices it already made):
+ *
+ *   php artisan stripe:sync --module-plans --publish
+ *
+ * A plan that is already sold (public or with a Stripe price) is left as its owner set it, and logged; a hidden, unpriced
+ * one under the same slug takes these terms and keeps only these products. Idempotent.
+ */
